  "4f0dae4e-69be-4f51-b042-28fec8aa5938": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p7-definition-block",
     "replacement": "We can define the absolute value as a **piecewise-defined function**:\n\n$$|x|=\\begin{cases}x,&\\text{for }x\\geq0,\\\\-x,&\\text{for }x<0\\end{cases}$$\n\nMore generally:\n\n$$|f(x)|=\\begin{cases}f(x),&\\text{for }f(x)\\geq0,\\\\-f(x),&\\text{for }f(x)<0\\end{cases}$$\n\nTo write an absolute value expression as a piecewise function:\n\n1. Write $|f(x)|=\\begin{cases}f(x),&\\text{for }f(x)\\geq0,\\\\-f(x),&\\text{for }f(x)<0\\end{cases}$\n2. Solve each condition for $x$ and simplify each branch.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p7-definition-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "ce3b02f08e9176117b69b4fec8c33e367b0f0bd07ed9f9de22ef353ecaaa0395"
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-working-with-functions-12-absolute-value-functions-v1/20261111-085300",
   "revision": 9
  },
  "bc13cc6a-fe3b-49cb-b375-2886479b61d3": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p10-theory-block",
     "replacement": "An **absolute value equation** has the form $|ax+b|=k$, where $a\\ne0$. If $k<0$, there is no solution, because an absolute value is never negative. If $k\\ge0$, remove the absolute value signs by considering two cases, using the fact that an expression and its negative have the same absolute value.\n\n$$|ax+b|=k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad|-(ax+b)|=k$$\n\n**Case 1: **$ax+b\\ge0$ $$ax+b=k$$\t**Case 2: **$ax+b<0$ $$-(ax+b)=k$$\n\nAvoid making a habit of writing\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|ax+b|&=k\\\\ax+b&=\\pm k\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAlthough this is valid for equations when $k\\ge0$, transferring this shortcut to inequalities can lead to mistakes. Use the two cases.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p10-theory-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "dac3d297abb0982024ed06117d8902e29235fc74013e01b99d12e47c60b3a1db"
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-working-with-functions-12-absolute-value-functions-v1/20261111-085300",
   "revision": 9
  },
  "bc9968e6-b15b-47c3-82ed-71edaa6da968": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p11-teaching",
     "replacement": "When $x$ appears on both sides, the algebra can produce an **extraneous solution**: a value that satisfies a case but not the original equation.\n\nAlways verify each solution by substituting it into the original equation.\n\nWe will see where extraneous solutions come from when we solve graphically.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p11-teaching",
     "targetFingerprint": "a46e1ee3daf37727d3dd8b682cbd31efe6d90cc748d07acbe03ae4644ab2452d",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-working-with-functions-12-absolute-value-functions-v1/20261111-085300",
   "revision": 9
  },
  "209a9809-2418-402a-b95e-eb870f4ebe7c": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p16-teaching",
     "replacement": "To find the equation of an absolute value graph, $y=|ax+b|$ for an upward V or $y=-|ax+b|$ for a downward V,\n\n1. Consider either straight-line branch.\n  Choose the branch where you have at least two points.\n2. Find its gradient $a$ and $y$-intercept $b$, giving the line $y=ax+b$.\n3. Write $y=|ax+b|$ if the V opens upward, or $y=-|ax+b|$ if it opens downward.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p16-teaching",
     "targetFingerprint": "93bea793a6835b25af4db5ae2a0e00f638ff0a369642e5f9c555ed338cc235f9",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-working-with-functions-12-absolute-value-functions-v1/20261111-085300",
